WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.679
Can Bitcoin really become the

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:03.299
world's reserve currency if it

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:05.219
doesn't displace the dollar, the

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:07.499
euro or the yuan as an everyday

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:08.969
medium of exchange?

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:11.159
Why isn't its price increasing

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:13.109
as inflation rises to levels

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:15.269
not seen in 40 years?

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:17.099
What sorts of regulations, if

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:18.959
any, would best facilitate

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.149
the wide scale adoption of Bitcoin,

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:23.099
not just among the wealthy but the

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:24.629
unbanked of the world?

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:26.069
Avik Roy is the president of the

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:27.959
Foundation for Research on Equal

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:30.059
Opportunity an Austin, Texas,

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:32.069
based think tank that focuses

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:34.109
on economic growth and mobility.

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:35.909
He says that Bitcoin improves on the

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:37.919
ability of gold as a non-state

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:39.779
backed store of value and has

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:41.759
shown itself to be effectively

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:43.979
uncensored revolt qualities that

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:45.375
will allow it to act as a game

00:00:45.376 --> 00:00:47.219
changing restraint on

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:49.199
the worst actions of governments

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:50.909
and central banks.

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:52.979
Reason spoke with him at the Bitcoin

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:54.689
conference in Miami in April.

00:01:00.389 --> 00:01:01.349
Avik Roy, thanks for talking to

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:02.248
Reason.

00:01:02.249 --> 00:01:03.209
Nick,it's always an honor.

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:05.068
So can

00:01:05.069 --> 00:01:07.769
Bitcoin become the standard

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:09.689
of reserve currency, the standard

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:10.799
payment system?

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:12.449
Well, I would say there's there's

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:13.709
there's a couple of ways to answer

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:14.909
that question.

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:17.339
And the short answer is yes.

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:18.959
But it's important, I think, to

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:20.729
understand that the traditional

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:23.069
conception of money is wrong.

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:24.959
So most people, if they've heard

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:26.279
a definition of money from an

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:28.769
economist, an academic economist,

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:29.909
they'll hear this definition which

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:31.079
goes something like this.

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:33.659
There's three attributes to money.

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:34.979
The first is that it has to be a

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:36.509
medium of exchange.

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:37.979
The second is that it's a unit of

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:39.389
account, meaning like you balance

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:41.249
your books on that on the

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:42.809
denomination of that currency.

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:44.069
And the third is that it's a store

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:45.209
of value, meaning you can put it in

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:47.789
the bank and know it's not going to

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:48.749
collapse in value.

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:49.589
Right.

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:51.419
And the the argument that you

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:53.069
hear a lot of academics make against

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:54.689
Bitcoin is that, well, it's not a

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:57.059
me, it's not a unit of account.

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:58.709
It's not really a medium of exchange

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:00.269
today.

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:01.949
It may be it's a store of value.

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:03.539
So it's one out of three.

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:05.399
Well, I would argue the US dollar

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:06.299
is only two out of three.

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:07.469
It's not really a good store of

00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:09.119
value today.

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:11.249
Leave aside the most recent

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:12.089
spurt of inflation.

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:13.559
If you just think about the 2%

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:15.479
paradigm that the Fed has been on

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:16.770
for the last 40 years.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:19.829
If you put your money in a bank at

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:21.899
0% interest rates

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:23.849
and it depreciated by 2% every

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:26.039
year in roughly

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:27.479
25 years, it would lose half of its

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:28.379
value.

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:29.489
That's not good.

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:31.589
Right. That's that is a punishment

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:32.729
to saving.

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:34.049
In fact, that's the whole point.

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:36.179
The whole Keynesian argument

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:38.519
as to why we have to have inflation

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:40.469
is because if we don't, people won't

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:41.849
be incentivized to spend their money

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:43.349
now instead of saving it.

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:44.429
So the whole idea is for the

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:46.199
government to steer you into

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:47.429
spending your money. So all that to

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:49.439
say that if

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:50.879
the argument is that the US dollar

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:52.829
is money and Bitcoin

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:54.779
is not because the US

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:56.099
dollar meets these three tests and

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:57.059
Bitcoin does not, that's not

00:02:57.060 --> 00:02:58.739
actually true because the US dollar

00:02:58.740 --> 00:02:59.819
only meets two of the three.

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:01.619
It's not a good store of value.

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:02.620
Now

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:05.009
taking it to the next level.

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:07.439
So Friedrich Hayek, among others,

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:09.359
makes the argument that the

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:11.069
way to think about money is not in

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:12.599
that sort of binary way of yes or

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:14.009
no. Does it meet these?

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:15.929
Can you check these three boxes?

00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:17.759
It's that all

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:19.589
forms of money exist

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:20.693
on a spectrum of moneyness in

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:23.339
each of these dimensions.

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:25.199
And different types of money can be

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:26.909
more useful in some dimensions

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:29.009
versus others. So, for example, some

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:30.839
forms of money may be more useful

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:32.339
as mediums of exchange because

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:33.839
they're, say, more widely accepted

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:34.859
by merchants.

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:36.629
So there's a network effect there.

00:03:36.630 --> 00:03:38.669
Some units of money may be more

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:40.499
useful as stores of value because

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:42.029
they don't get inflated away like

00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:43.139
Bitcoin. Right.

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:44.999
So if you think about money in

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:47.099
those dimensions, meaning like

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:48.839
different types of clothes that you

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:50.459
would wear some to a dinner party,

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:52.769
some to a bitcoin conference,

00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:53.770
some to a Reason dinner,

00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:56.279
different kinds of money can have

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:57.209
different purposes.

00:03:57.210 --> 00:03:59.249
And I would say that where

00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:01.439
Bitcoin is most dominant

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:02.459
is as a store of value.

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:04.499
It seems pretty clear today.

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:07.169
The writing is on the wall that

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:08.489
and I argued this in a piece I wrote

00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:10.409
for National Affairs last year

00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:13.139
that Bitcoin is destined

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:15.509
to transcend the US Treasury

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:17.338
bond as a store

00:04:17.339 --> 00:04:19.889
of value, which is very important.

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:21.929
It's not as important whether

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:24.209
or not the US dollar or Bitcoin

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:25.659
is the dominant medium of exchange.

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:27.689
I'll give you an example. I was in

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:29.049
Portugal for a conference a couple

00:04:29.050 --> 00:04:30.929
of weeks ago. I never drew

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:32.699
a single euro out of an ATM.

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:34.589
I never paid with paper, currency or

00:04:34.590 --> 00:04:36.359
coins in euros for anything.

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:37.979
I used my American issued credit

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:39.569
cards for every single transaction I

00:04:39.570 --> 00:04:41.399
made and the exchange between

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:42.659
the US dollar in a year all happened

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:43.949
on the back end, right?

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:45.719
That can happen with bitcoin too.

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:47.819
If you have a bank account,

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:49.949
quote unquote, whether it's a wallet

00:04:49.950 --> 00:04:51.869
or something that's custody, that's

00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:53.939
based denominated in Bitcoin and

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:55.889
you're spending money off of that

00:04:55.890 --> 00:04:57.959
with a USD accepting

00:04:57.960 --> 00:04:59.639
merchant that can do all that.

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:01.049
All of that conversion can happen on

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:02.369
the back end. You don't have to

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:04.439
really care. So this whole idea

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:05.549
of a medium of exchange being

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:07.379
important, I actually don't think

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:08.189
it's that important.

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:09.659
I think it's overstated both by

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:11.489
bitcoiners and non bitcoiners,

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:13.589
what really matters and

00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:14.969
what's really going to create the

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:16.469
social transformation of this

00:05:16.470 --> 00:05:18.089
century for both good and for ill in

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:20.129
terms of the disruption will cause?

00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:21.959
Is Bitcoin being a better store of

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:23.490
value than the US dollar?

